			"date": "2023-05-18T14:27:04.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@autiomaa\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>autiomaa</span></a></span> I feel <a href=\"https://attractive.space/tags/Javascript\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">#<span>Javascript</span></a> frameworks often just don't fit into my way of thinking, forcing me to code in their style, rather than my preferred solution for the issue. In addition they become bloated with layers upon layers.</p><p>I built my own JS framework, <a href=\"https://attractive.space/tags/storkCore\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">#<span>storkCore</span></a>, to pretty much be the opposite of that. Slowly adding bits and pieces over the years.</p><p><a href=\"https://chiselapp.com/user/Setok/repository/storkCore/index\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">chiselapp.com/user/Setok/repos</span><span class=\"invisible\">itory/storkCore/index</span></a></p><p>Though I guess you could see that it, too, has its own philosophy. It fits my thought model, though.</p>",
